When I booked two weeks on a turtle conservation project in southern Greece, I imagined long, lazy days on the beach and the occasional photograph of a baby turtle. My friends, who know me better than that, gave me a week before I would be on the first flight home.

{{31}}

Our job was to walk the two kilometres of beach before sunrise, looking for the wide, scuffed trails that female loggerhead turtles leave when they haul themselves ashore at night. Each trail had to be followed to the nest, which we marked with a numbered stake and protected with a wire cage against foxes.

{{32}}

Dimitra, the project's coordinator, had a remedy for this. She showed us how to push a thin rod into the sand and feel for the looser, damper soil where a nest had been filled in. It took me four days to feel the difference, and when I finally did, I almost shouted with delight.

{{33}}

We sat thirty metres away for nearly an hour, hardly daring to breathe, while she dug, laid and covered her eggs with her back flippers. When she finally dragged herself back to the water, nobody spoke. I had to wipe my eyes with my sleeve.

{{34}}

The ones who arrive in August, however, have jobs of their own. They guard the nests at night, and when the hatchlings emerge they watch the beach to make sure the tiny turtles head for the sea rather than towards the bright lights of the nearby hotels.

{{35}}

This summer, for the first time, six of the eight hotels have agreed, and the beach is noticeably darker at night. Dimitra describes it as the project's greatest success, and she says it came not from rules but from patient conversations, many of them started by volunteers who stayed on to chat with the staff.

{{36}}
